    "case_summary": "A 41-year-old woman originally from Sierra Leone, living in Boston. Recent history: SARS-CoV-2 infection 2 weeks prior with anosmia and cough lasting 4 days. Three days before presentation, she developed fever, headache, and malaise. Past medical history includes malaria as a child. Travel history: Last visited Sierra Leone 19 years ago; visited Nigeria and Dominican Republic 5 years ago (took mefloquine prophylaxis in Nigeria, no prophylaxis in Dominican Republic). Current occupation: hospital facilities management. Lives with 3-year-old daughter in an apartment. No history of blood transfusions, injection drug use, or recent international visitors.\n\nTemperature: 39.4˚CRegular tachycardiaBlood pressure: normalRespiration: normalLung sounds: clearNo splenomegalyNo jaundiceNo neurologic deficits\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Leukocyte count: 7.5 × 10⁹/L (normal)- Platelet count: 90 × 10⁹/L (thrombocytopenia)- Blood chemistry, liver and kidney function: normalImaging:- Chest radiograph: normalParasitology Tests:- Thick blood smear: Plasmodium species present- Rapid diagnostic testing (Abbot BinaxNOW): positive for T1 (histidine-rich protein 2) and T2 (aldolase) antigens- Peripheral blood smear: 3.5% parasitemia- Serum polymerase chain reaction testing (ARUP Laboratories)Images:Figure 1 Title: Thin smear, Wright-Giemsa stainFigure 1 Description: Delicate ring forms (trophozoites) and a multiply infected erythrocyte with appliqué forms, 1000× magnificationFigure 2 Title: Thin smear, Wright-Giemsa stainFigure 2 Description: Occasional ovalocytes containing trophozoites with larger chromatin dots and slightly more cytoplasm, 1000× magnification",
